@page "/counter"

@* Two click paths to compare IC latency profiles:

   • "Click me (Blazor Server)" — @onclick fires through the circuit:
     blazor.web.js → /_blazor POST (IC update call, ~2 s consensus) →
     CircuitHubFacade dispatches → IncrementCount → render diff → next
     /_blazor poll. End-to-end 5–15 s including poll cycle. This is
     authentic Blazor Server semantics on a decentralised replica.

   • "Fast click (query RPC)" — JS waspFastClick() in App.razor calls
     /api/click?c=N (IC query call, ~50 ms RTT). No consensus, no
     persistence — the count is tracked client-side. Demonstrates the
     fastest user-visible IC interaction.

   The slow button only becomes responsive once the SignalR Long
   Polling handshake completes (~13 s after page load — visible as
   #blazor-status in App.razor). The fast button works immediately. *@

@* id="wasp-count" is required so the wasp-bridge.js post-click count
   restore (CircuitHubFacade._wapsClickCount + JS.BeginInvokeJS
   waspSetCount) can find this span. *@
<p role="status">Current count: <span id="wasp-count">@currentCount</span></p>

<div style="display:flex; gap:0.75rem; flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top:0.5rem;">
    <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="IncrementCount">
        Click me (Blazor Server, 5–15 s)
    </button>
    <button class="btn" type="button" style="background:#16a34a;"
            onclick="waspFastClick()">
        Fast click (query RPC, ~50 ms)
    </button>
</div>

<p id="wasp-fast-status" style="margin-top:0.75rem; color:#64748b; font-size:0.9rem;">
    Click the green button to compare latencies.
</p>

@code {
    private int currentCount = 0;

    private void IncrementCount()
    {
        currentCount++;
    }
}
